<GuidePage title="The App Router Guide - Craft Education" root="." cover>
<div class="kicker">Craft Education · Engineering</div>
<h1>The App Router Guide</h1>
<p class="lede">
  How to build with Next.js and React the way they are designed to be used: server-first.
  Patterns for composition, data fetching, streaming, and the client/server boundary, and
  the goals behind them.
</p>

<Callout kind="voice" title="Why this document exists">
  <p>
    The architecture and approach we use now will set the stage for the rest of the
    product's life. This guide is the shared mental model: what we optimize for on every
    page, the patterns that get us there, and the signs we've drifted. Read the docs it
    links to; this is the map, they are the territory.
  </p>
</Callout>

<h2 style="margin-top: 36px">The thesis</h2>
<p>
  On every page, your job is to
  <strong>separate what can be known at first render from what cannot</strong>; then
  <strong>maximize the known</strong>, <strong>contain the unknown</strong>, and
  <strong>decouple the parts</strong>. Everything in this guide is in service of one of
  those three goals. Server-side rendering matters because it makes the "known" surface
  dramatically larger than a browser-only app can ever make it.
</p>

<h2>How to read this document</h2>
<p>
  Every practice in this guide is tagged twice: by <strong>scope</strong> (does it apply
  to all React, or specifically to Next.js?) and by <strong>importance</strong> (how much
  it matters that we follow it).
</p>
<div class="legend">
  <div class="legend-box">
    <h4>Scope</h4>
    <div class="legend-row">
      <Chip k="react" /><span class="desc"
        >Applies to any React application, with or without Next.js.</span
      >
    </div>
    <div class="legend-row">
      <Chip k="next" /><span class="desc"
        >Specific to, or dramatically amplified by, the App Router.</span
      >
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="legend-box">
    <h4>Importance</h4>
    <div class="legend-row">
      <Chip k="critical" /><span class="desc"
        >Foundational. Getting this wrong undermines the architecture itself; deviate only
        with a specific, articulable reason; legitimate exceptions are named where they
        exist.</span
      >
    </div>
    <div class="legend-row">
      <Chip k="very" /><span class="desc"
        >Our default on every page. Occasional exceptions expected; use judgment and be
        ready to explain the deviation.</span
      >
    </div>
    <div class="legend-row">
      <Chip k="imp" /><span class="desc"
        >A meaningful improvement worth adopting as habit; lowest ceremony to deviate
        from.</span
      >
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

